    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (the only runnable test class is `AcceptanceTest`; the other files are the base and a helper).\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented `namesakeCount` on the create-owner flow: `POST /api/owners` now records, and `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns, the number of pre-existing owners sharing the same `firstName` and `lastName` compared case-insensitively.\n\nChanges, following the OfficeFloor function-injection conventions in AGENTS.md:\n\n1. **New function step** \u2014 `rest/function/owner/CountNamesakes.java`: reads the built `Owner` via `@Val`, counts existing owners (`ownerRepository.findAll()`) matching first+last name case-insensitively, and mutates `owner.setNamesakeCount(...)` in place. It runs before `save`, so the new owner isn't counted.\n\n2. **Pipeline wiring** \u2014 `officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`: inserted a `countNamesakes` step (under `transaction` governance) between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`, mirroring the existing `AssignCustomerCode` pattern.\n\n3. **Persistence** \u2014 added a `namesake_count` column to the `owners` table in all four schemas (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) and a mapped `namesakeCount` field (with getter/setter) on `model/Owner.java`, so the value survives to the later `GET`, exactly like `customerCode`/`householdId`.\n\n4. **API contract** \u2014 added the read-only `namesakeCount` (int32, min 0) property to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml`, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it automatically.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes` (two owners sharing `firstName`+`lastName`, different addresses) passes with `namesakeCount == 1` on the second owner, and `BUILD SUCCESS` overall.",
